<Base
  title="Neglected Pool Recovery: What It Really Involves"
  description="Inherited a green pool from a new home purchase or a rental turnover? What an honest green-to-clean recovery timeline and process looks like. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Opening a neglected pool: what a green-to-clean recovery really involves</h1>

  <p>
    Recovering a fully neglected pool typically takes 3 to 7 days from first test to clear water,
    and it always starts with testing, not treatment — specifically checking stabilizer (CYA)
    before any shock goes in, since a pool that's been sitting untreated for weeks often has
    chemistry too far out of range for chlorine to work until that's addressed first.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Inherited a green or neglected pool" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>Who this usually happens to</h2>
  <p>
    New homeowners inheriting a pool the previous owner let slide before selling, landlords
    between tenants, and — especially around Fort Cavazos in Killeen and Harker Heights — military
    families dealing with a pool left untreated during a PCS move are the three situations we see
    most. None of these are your fault, and none of them mean the pool is ruined; they just mean
    the recovery starts from a different point than routine maintenance.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest first step: test before you treat</h2>
  <p>
    It's tempting to dump in shock the moment you see green water, but that's often money wasted.
    If stabilizer has built up over a previous owner's years of trichlor tabs, chlorine may be
    chemically unable to sanitize the water no matter the dose — see our guide on{' '}
    <a href="/guides/pool-shock-not-working-cya/">why shocking a pool sometimes fails outright</a>.
    A full water test up front tells you whether this is a straightforward shock-and-brush job or
    one that needs a partial drain before treatment can even start working.
  </p>

  <h2>What a typical recovery looks like</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Day 1 — test and plan.</strong> Full chemistry test including CYA, visual
      inspection of the equipment, and an honest timeline based on how far gone the water is.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Day 1–2 — correct chemistry.</strong> Partial drain if CYA requires it, then
      balancing pH and alkalinity so chlorine treatment has a fair chance to work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Day 2–5 — shock and brush cycle.</strong> Repeated shock dosing paired with
      brushing every surface, running filtration around the clock rather than on a normal
      schedule to keep up with the chemical demand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Day 5–7 — clear-up and fine filtration.</strong> Dead algae and fine particulate
      take the filter longer to clear than the chemical fight itself; this stage is often more
      about patience and run time than additional treatment.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The "drain it all and start over" question</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes a full drain is genuinely the right call — extremely high CYA, structural algae
    staining, or water so far gone that partial measures won't catch up fast enough. But a full
    drain isn't automatically safer or faster, and on some pool shells it introduces real risk
    from hydrostatic groundwater pressure pushing up on an empty shell. That decision is worth a
    professional look rather than a default assumption either way.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    If you're dealing with a fresh green pool and want to try a shock-and-brush cycle yourself,
    testing CYA first (many basic kits don't include it) will tell you whether that plan can
    actually work. Call in a technician if the pool has clearly been neglected for a month or
    more, if you don't have a way to test CYA, or if a full or partial drain is on the table — the
    risk of doing that step wrong is higher than the risk of any other stage in recovery.
    {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>We just closed on a house and the pool is green. Is it always fixable?</h3>
    <p>
      Nearly always, yes — a green pool is a chemistry and cleaning problem, not usually a sign
      the pool itself is damaged. Structural or equipment damage is a separate question worth
      checking during the same visit, but green water on its own is recoverable.
    </p>
    <h3>How much does a full recovery typically cost compared to routine cleaning?</h3>
    <p>
      As a general industry range rather than a quoted price, full recoveries commonly cost
      several times a single routine cleaning visit, since they involve multiple days of labor,
      more chemical, and sometimes a partial drain. See our{' '}
      <a href="/guides/pool-cleaning-cost-central-texas/">pool cleaning cost guide</a> for more
      context.
    </p>
    <h3>Can the pool be used at all during recovery?</h3>
    <p>
      Not safely until chemistry is back in a normal, balanced range — the shock levels used
      during active recovery are well above what's safe for swimming, and algae-fighting
      chemistry isn't typical pool water.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Inherited a green or neglected pool" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
